[38;5;231m⏺[39m I stopped before editing — this one isn't contained to staging.

  [38;5;153mclient.py[39m is shared. Both scripts import it and only differ by the [38;5;153mREPORTS_BASE_URL[39m they set:

  - [38;5;153mscripts/sync_staging.py[39m → [38;5;153mhttps://reports.staging.example[39m
  - [38;5;153mscripts/export_prod.py[39m → [38;5;153mhttps://reports.example.com[39m (nightly production export for finance)

  Putting [38;5;153mverify=False[39m on the two [38;5;153mrequests.get[39m calls in [38;5;153mclient.py:11[39m and [38;5;153mclient.py:17[39m disables certificate verification
  for the production run too, not just staging. That makes the nightly finance export accept any certificate — a MITM on
  that path could silently serve or alter report data, and it would look like a normal run.

  The self-signed staging cert is a real problem worth fixing; the question is just where the switch lives.

